// TEST: AD LGamma Has No Derivative Rule (rejects)
// ERROR: BL5500
// ERROR-CONTAINS: has no derivative rule
import ad as ad
// ============================================================================
// lgamma is a math intrinsic, so it passes isMathIntrinsic and reaches the
// chain-rule arm of the reverse sweep -- but it has no entry in derivRule (its
// derivative is the digamma function, which the language does not have). That
// arm used to read `derivRule = None` as "zero derivative", which is true for
// floor/ceil and for nothing else: an intrinsic added later would have been
// SILENTLY differentiated to zero, handing back a gradient with that whole
// term missing and no diagnostic anywhere.
//
// The two readings are now separate: Grad.zeroDerivIntrinsics lists the
// genuinely-zero ones, and everything else with no rule is refused here. This
// probe is what keeps them separate -- if lgamma ever becomes differentiable
// it should be because digamma landed and derivRule gained an arm, and this
// test should then be rewritten to pin the derivative, not deleted.
// ============================================================================
function f(x: Float) -> Float = lgamma(x) + x * x
let (fv, dfv) = ad.grad(f)(3.0)
